<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Peach</h1></b><br>

    Growing peaches in the UK is entirely possible with careful variety selection and a little extra attention. Peaches thrive best in the <strong>warmer, sheltered parts of southern England</strong> but can also be successfully grown in <strong>greenhouses, conservatories, or against sunny walls</strong> further north. Thanks to climate change and new breeding techniques, peaches are becoming increasingly popular among UK gardeners.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Best Growing Conditions</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Climate:</strong> Peaches need warm, dry summers and protection from late spring frosts.<br>
    - <strong>Soil:</strong> Well-drained, fertile soil with a neutral to slightly acidic pH (6.0–6.5).<br>
    - <strong>Sunlight:</strong> Full sun is essential — ideally a south or south-west facing position.<br>
    - <strong>Shelter:</strong> A wall, fence, or greenhouse helps protect peaches from harsh winds and cold snaps.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Common Peach Diseases in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Bacterial Spot:</strong> Small, dark, water-soaked lesions on leaves and fruit. Severe infections can cause defoliation and fruit blemishes. Reduce risk by selecting resistant varieties, avoiding overhead watering, and removing infected leaves.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Tips for Growing Peaches in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Choose early varieties:</strong> ‘Avalon Pride’, ‘Peregrine’, and ‘Redhaven’ are good options for the UK climate.<br>
    - <strong>Hand-pollinate:</strong> In cooler springs, help pollination by gently transferring pollen between flowers using a soft brush.<br>
    - <strong>Frost protection:</strong> Cover flowering trees with horticultural fleece during frosty nights.<br>
    - <strong>Prune carefully:</strong> Prune after harvesting to encourage good air circulation and maintain tree health.<br>
    - <strong>Monitor watering:</strong> Keep soil moist but not waterlogged, especially during fruit development.<br><br>

    With the right care and attention, peaches can bring a touch of the Mediterranean to UK gardens, rewarding growers with sweet, juicy fruits each summer.
    </i></p>
